The test scenario:

Journey steps:
1. Seek selector "#reminder". That's likely the first radio? Actually index.html shows two groups: channel-radios group includes inputs with ids "reminder", "reminder-2"? Wait check: In index.html snippet:

```
<div class="govuk-radios govuk-radios--conditional" data-module="govuk-radios" id="channel-radios">
  <div class="govuk-radios__item">
    <input class="govuk-radios__input" id="reminder" name="reminder" type="radio" value="email" data-aria-controls="conditional-reminder">
    <label class="govuk-label govuk-radios__label" for="reminder">Email</label>
  </div>
  ...
```

So first input id=reminder (value email), second input id=reminder-2 (value text). Then later outside that group but still within same form: `<div class="govuk-radios" data-module="govuk-radios" id="optout-radios"><div class="govuk-radios__item"><input ... id="reminder-none"...>` So three radios total sharing name="reminder".

Thus step sequence: seek #reminder selects the email radio (first). Then press ArrowDown moves focus to next radio in DOM order: which is #reminder-2 (the text message). After pressing ArrowDown again, it goes to #reminder-none (opt-out). At that point expectation: visible #conditional-reminder-2 false (mobile number field hidden). However observed: after moving to #reminder-none, #conditional-reminder-2 remains visible (i.e., not hidden). Let's examine why.

Observations: When on #reminder-2 (text message) initially, its associated conditional (#conditional-reminder-2) was shown because the radio had attribute `aria-controls="conditional-reminder-2"` and presumably when checked sets aria-expanded true and toggles class.

But note: The initial selection at start may be something else? The journey begins by seeking #reminder (email). Not sure what is initially selected/focused. But after first ArrowDown, focus moved to #reminder-2 (text). It didn't say any assertions about visibility after first arrow down; maybe they expected conditional-reminder-2 becomes visible? They didn't assert. Anyway after second ArrowDown focusing #reminder-none, they expect conditional-reminder-2 hidden.

Why does it stay visible? Likely because the radios script only handles click events, not keyboard events (arrow keys). The radios component listens for 'click' on the module, and when a radio with aria-controls is clicked, it updates attributes (aria-expanded and toggles class). However keyboard navigation changes focus but does not change checked state unless user presses Space or Enter to select. In many radio groups, arrow keys change focus but also automatically change the checked state (moving focus changes which radio is selected). Actually native behavior: Using arrow keys to navigate radios changes the checked state (i.e., selects the newly focused radio). Indeed, in HTML, moving focus via arrow keys among radios with same name will change the checked property. Let’s verify: Typically, when a radio group receives focus, arrow keys move focus and also change the checked state to the newly focused radio. Yes, that is standard behavior. So after pressing ArrowDown twice, the third radio (#reminder-none) should become checked, and the previously checked radio (#reminder-2) becomes unchecked. The script should respond to change in checked state to hide/show conditionals. However the script only reacts to click events, not to change events. Since the checked state changed via keyboard interaction, the script doesn't run setAttributes to update aria-expanded and toggle class. Therefore the conditional remains visible (since it was shown when #reminder-2 was checked, and never hidden when it became unchecked).

Thus root cause: Missing handling of change events (or key events) that alter checked state; only click handled.

Repair strategy: Modify the Radios component to also listen for 'change' events on the radios (or perhaps 'keyup' for arrow keys?) Actually easiest: add event listener for 'change' on $module (the radios container) and call setAttributes for the changed input. Or we could modify handleClick to also handle change. Since the radios already have logic to set attributes based on checked state, we can just add a change listener.

Implementation: In init(), after adding click listener, also add $module.addEventListener('change', this.handleChange.bind(this)); where handleChange similar to handleClick but loops through inputs and if input has aria-controls, call setAttributes.

Alternatively, we could reuse handleClick as it works for any input with aria-controls regardless of event type (click vs change). So we can rename handleClick to handleInputEvent and attach to both click and change.

